</text>
Reason:
This statement frees the array `mixer->id_elems` while the `kctl` elements (controls) that were added to the card still reference memory within that array. When `snd_card_register()` later triggers the OSS mixer layer callbacks, those callbacks access the freed `id_elems`, causing a use-after-free read.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
<text>
for (id = 0; id < MAX_ID_ELEMS; id++) {
    for (list = mixer->id_elems[id]; list; list = next) {
        next = list->next_id_elem;
        if (list->kctl)
            snd_ctl_remove(mixer->chip->card, list->kctl);
    }
}
kfree(mixer->id_elems);
</text>
Reason:
The loop iterates over all elements in `mixer->id_elems` and calls `snd_ctl_remove()` for each control (`kctl`) before freeing the array. This ensures that the controls are unregistered from the card, so they no longer reference the soon-to-be-freed memory. The `next` pointer is saved before removal because `snd_ctl_remove()` may free the current list element. Only after all controls are removed is `mixer->id_elems` safely freed.

